P:Chorus
"Am"e2e "G"d2B "Am"A3 | "Am"E2A A2A "G"Bc d/d/ | "Am"e2e "G"d2B "Am"A2c | "G"B2A G2A z3 | "Am"e2d "G"B2G "Am"A3 |]
w: One, two, three, four, five, Hunt the hare and turn her down the rock-y road and all the way to Dub-lin, Whack fol-lol-de-rah!
W:
W:In the merry month of June, when first from home I started,
W:And left the girls alone, sad and broken-hearted.
W:Shook hands with father dear, kissed my darling mother,
W:Drank a pint of beer, my tears and grief to smother;
W:Then off to reap the corn, and leave where I was born.
W:I cut a stout black-thorn to banish ghost or goblin;
W:With a pair of bran new brogues, I rattled o'er the bogs —
W:Sure I frightened all the dogs on the rocky road to Dublin.
W:
W:(Chorus) One, two, three, four five,
W:Hunt the hare and turn her
W:Down the rocky road
W:And all the ways to Dublin,
W:Whack-fol-lol-de-ra.
W:
W:In Mullingar, that night, I rested limbs so weary.
W:Started by daylight, with spirits light and airy;
W:Took a drop of the pure, to keep my spirits from sinking,
W:That's always an Irishman's cure, whenever he's troubled with thinking.
W:To see the lassies smile, laughing all the while
W:At my comical style, set my heart a-bubbling.
W:They axed if I was hired, the wages I required.
W:Until I was almost tired of the rocky road to Dublin.
W:
W:In Dublin next arrived, I thought it was a pity
W:To be so soon deprived of a view of that fine city;
W:'Twas then I took a stroll, all among the quality,
W:My bundle then was stole in a neat locality,
W:Something crossed my mind, thinks I, I'll look behind.
W:No bundle could I find upon my stick a-wobbling.
W:Inquiring for the rogue, they said my Connaught brogue.
W:It wasn't much in vogue on the rocky road to Dublin.
W:
W:A coachman raised his hand as if myself was wanting,
W:I went up to a stand, full of cars for jaunting;
W:"Step up, my boy!" says he; "Ah, that I will with pleasure,"
W:"And to the Strawberry Beds, I'll drive you at your leisure."
W:"A strawberry bed?" says I, "faith, that would be too high!"
W:"On one of straw I'll lie, and the berries won't be troubling";
W:He drove me out as far, upon an outside car.
W:Faith! such jolting never wor on the rocky road to Dublin.
W:
W:In Dublin next arrived, I thought it such a pity,
W:To be so soon deprived, A view of that fine city.
W:Then I took a stroll, All among the quality,
W:My bundle it was stole, In a neat locality;
W:Something crossed my mind, Then I looked behind;
W:No bundle could I find, Upon my stick a wobblin’.
W:Enquirin’ for the rogue, They said my Connacht brogue,
W:Wasn’t much in vogue, On the rocky road to Dublin.
W:
W:I soon got out of that, my spirits never failing,
W:I landed on the quay, just as the ship was sailing.
W:The captain at me roared, swore that no room had he.
W:But when I leaped on board, they a cabin found for Paddy.
W:Down among the pigs I played such rummy rigs,
W:Danced some hearty jigs, with water round me bubbling.
W:But when off Holyhead, I wished that I was dead,
W:Or safely put in bed, on the rocky road to Dublin.
W:
W:The boys in Liverpool, when on the dock I landed.
W:Called myself a fool, I could no longer stand it;
W:My blood began to boil, my temper I was losing.
W:And poor old Erin's Isle, they all began abusing.
W:"Hurrah! my boys," says I, my shillelagh I let fly.
W:Some Galway boys were by, they saw I was a hobbling;
W:Then with a loud "hurrah!" they joined me in the fray.
W:Faugh-a-ballagh! Clear the way! for the rocky road to Dublin!
W:
W:For it is the rocky road, here's the road to Dublin;
W:Here's the rocky road, now fire away to Dublin !
W:The steam-coach was at hand, the driver said he'd cheap ones.
W:But sure the luggage van was too much for my ha'pence.
W:For England I was bound, it would never do to balk it.
W:For every step of the road, bedad I says I, I'll walk it.
W:I did not sigh or moan until I saw Athlone.
W:A pain in my shin bone, it set my heart a-bubbling;
W:And fearing the big cannon, looking o'er the Shannon,
I W:very quickly ran on the rocky road to Dublin.